t(51, 2060, """On the occasion of the nearing end of the English occupation, if Allah Most High wills, soon

From the old traditional songs whose origin is the cry of a wife whose husband, the lion, did not return with the other lions .. In the days of the revolt of the people of Palestine against the English occupation between 1936 and 1939, the rebels would mount horses and launch attacks on the English forces, and return to their villages after each raid. If one of the rebels was martyred and did not return with the other rebels, his wife, as was the custom, would receive his rifle from one of the returning fighters, then sing:

The rifle appeared and the lion did not. O muzzle of the rifle, wet with his blood. Farewell, where are you going .. farewell, O musk spreading its scent ..""")

h(52, 400, """The rifle appeared, and the lion did not""")

h(52, 1700, """Hallow the King and magnify Him; there is no king but He""")

t(52, 2460, """A few minutes ago I read a comment by a follower replying to another follower's comment, telling him or her: do such and such. This "such" may cost 30 years and 60 years of torment. So my advice to people: do not draw conclusions, because we do not know the full stories of others! We see and read one end of a story! A follower's comment in which we read his or her pain; but""")

t(53, 60, """the whole story in its details does not appear from a single sentence a follower writes in a comment! There are terrifying stories, stories the mind cannot imagine. So personally I am very wary, whether here or in reality, and I never ever do it, of saying to a person: why don't you do such?! What if not doing it was because of a lifetime lost, a great hardship that lasted years, a health that was struck, or a psychological security lost for long years?! What do we know about one another that we read the comment of a sad person and tell him or her, with the effort of pressing a great button we think extraordinary: do good to the one who made your life a real hell, not imagined or probable, but a real hell? We press on the phone's letters, while the sad one has been crushed by mountains of rock that showed no mercy. Between a keyboard in our hands and huge rocks that never struck our chests, we very easily intrude on a person's pain, tears and life story of which we know nothing!""")

h(53, 900, """There are tragedies about which it is not right for a person to draw conclusions. How many an oppressed person is told: why don't you do good to the one who wronged you and ruined your life and wrecked you?!""")

t(53, 1730, """The wording "he is pledged allegiance to under compulsion, unwilling" did not come in a hadith with a sound chain connected to the Messenger ﷺ.

I agree with the scholars in what they said: "Nevertheless, the meaning taken from the body of narrations (even if the wording 'compelled, unwilling' is not authentic as attributed to the Prophet ﷺ and his family) is that the true Mahdi does not seek leadership for himself, and does not fight people so they pledge allegiance to him, as is the case of the false claimants to Mahdi-hood; rather, the people of authority gather around him and pledge allegiance to him while he holds back from the office and has no desire for it."
Here end the words of the knowers, and I find no error in them; rather I agree with them. But they did not notice another matter that supports refusal: unwillingness means refusal; there is no unwillingness together with acceptance and consent!

As for what none of the speakers and talkers spoke of, which only a person ((with personal experience and an intellectual vision based on the experience of others)) understands: the personal experience is when you set up a business project with a stranger who bears the look of religiosity, then he does every devilish thing to monopolize the decision, or to share with you in every decision you make, so the partnership becomes the worst decision you made in your life; whoever has experienced this will understand very well what I say, for here is born a work system that is among the ugliest partnership systems among humans, absolutely.

And the experience of others that builds in a person thought and a deep vision of life is the experience of Morsi, may Allah Most High have mercy on him: rising to power with the support of "others," his group = a very likely model for the 7, then they turned him into a mere facade and image, without being a real president. This is something very old and deep-rooted in human thinking. Let the reader, and whoever is deeply versed in history, look into the experiences of states, nations and revolutions; he will always find that there is""")

t(54, 60, """a faction that says: were it not for me, this would not have happened, and then works to snatch power and control the reins of affairs. And a ship with more than one captain will surely sink. What the commentators did not go deep into is the psychological act working on the natural, normal human soul with deep life experiences, not the foolish, trivial, cheap soul that seeks its own desires even if the price is impotence, smallness and lowliness, like the case of the small pretenders who do not mind being the model of Morsi, may Allah have mercy on him, who was in complete submission to those who brought him there and was unable to break free from them. These pretenders do not know, in the matter they have fallen into, an aspect that is among the most dangerous aspects: the aspect of the changes that befall a person. Even the righteous may deviate; even one who spent his lifetime loving his Ummah may deviate; even one who stood firm on the truth his whole life and strove for the good of his Ummah may deviate. And the seven are humans among humans: they have their minds, their experiences, their intellectual beliefs, their convictions, and souls that are purified or corrupted; their state is that of all the sons of Adam, each of them has his companion and his devil, and each has a soul that contends with its owner. I know the extent of naivety among the dervishes in handling this when they read the reports, and I do not say the hadiths: they look at the 7 as infallible prophets, or as righteous men sealed with the best of endings whose endings are guaranteed. To these dervishes I say: mature in order to understand, learn in order to know, and give up the swelling inside yourselves in order to grasp. For we see a discourse of delusion at times and of guardianship at other times, especially this guardianship when it appears in these people's recordings, which provokes much laughter; and both factions are dervishes. The one deluded by dozens of delusions is a dervish, and the one who convinced himself that he has guardianship is also a dervish. So the matter is not exactly as you have portrayed it to people over the last 15 years, when you presented them a poetic, romantic novel about 7 guaranteed angels with no souls contending with them; yet not all of them or half of them are rushing toward the model of Morsi, may Allah have mercy on him. The distance between dervishness and real understanding is the distance of the opening of the heart, the distance of humility before Allah Most High, and the distance of realizing that there is no knower above Allah's knowledge.

End of the explanation. For scholarly benefit, here is an explanation of the weakness of the wording (compelled - unwilling), and of the error of attributing this wording to the Prophet ﷺ and his family.
---------------------------
The details and grades of the hadith narrations on this matter are summarized as follows:
1. The famous narration with the wording "while he is unwilling" (a weak hadith)
The hadith people usually cite is the hadith of Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet ﷺ said:
"There will be a dispute at the death of a caliph, and a man of the people of Medina will go out fleeing to Mecca. People of Mecca will come to him and bring him out while he is unwilling, and pledge allegiance to him between the Corner and the Station..."

* Ruling on the chain: this hadith has a weak chain. Abu Dawud and Ahmad narrated it, and its chain has defects, among them the break between Qatadah and Abu al-Khalil, and some narrators in some of its routes were judged weak (such as Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an). Therefore a group of verifiers and hadith scholars, such as Imam al-Dhahabi and Sheikh al-Albani, judged it weak.
* There is another narration with the wording "while he is unwilling" via Qatadah, mursal (and mursal is among the kinds of weak).

2. The wording "compelled" in transmitted reports (not a Prophetic hadith)
The wording "he is pledged allegiance to by force" or "compelled" did not come attributed to the Prophet ﷺ; it came only in reports stopping at the words of some Companions or Followers. An example is what was narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he said: "... they will bring him out from the heart of Mecca ... and pledge allegiance to him by force," and these reports establish no proof attributed to the Messenger ﷺ.

3. The authentic hadiths established on this topic
The authentic hadiths established in the Sunnah that point to this general meaning (without the mentioned wording) are:

* The hadith of the army swallowed up at al-Bayda' (Sahih Muslim): in it, an army raiding the Sacred House is swallowed up by the earth. In a narration by Ahmad and al-Nasa'i, authenticated by al-Albani: "... they send (an army) to a man who comes to Mecca, and Allah protects him from them and they are swallowed up ... among them is one who is compelled, and he comes compelled." (What is meant by the compelled one here is a man of the raiding army forced to go out with them, not Imam al-Mahdi.)
* The hadith of the pledge (sound chain): from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ said: "Allegiance will be pledged to a man between the Corner and the Station..." (narrated by Ahmad and Ibn Hibban with a sound chain, and it does not contain the wording unwilling or compelled).""")
